  <section id="planner" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Feature-based planning</h2>
    <p>The planner sequences a prismatic part the way a CAM programmer does, from the features the parametric history really has:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>face the stock top</li>
      <li>rough around the outlines and inside the pockets (adaptive or contour, with a wall allowance)</li>
      <li>counterbores, spot drilling, drilling or helical boring, countersinks, thread milling</li>
      <li>finish the walls</li>
      <li>break the top edges with a 2D chamfer when deburring is asked</li>
      <li>3D roughing and finishing of freeform bodies</li>
    </ol>
    <p>A body without a feature history (a mesh, a Boolean of primitives, a sculpted part) is machined with the 3D surface strategies. Planning chooses tools and regions only; cutting data is computed per operation when the job runs. A real run on a 44 × 44 × 11 mm POM-C blank planned three operations: <code>face</code> with facemill-50, <code>surface-rough</code> with flat-16 and <code>surface-finish</code> with ball-10, released as <code>verified-program</code> with a 149 s cycle time. That run is drawn in <a href="/docs/guides/native-manufacturing">native manufacturing</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="cutters" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Cutters and the tool assembly</h2>
    <p>The default library has 70 cutters of nine kinds: flat (Ø1–16, plus aluminium ZrN variants), ball (Ø1–10), bull nose, face mill, spot drill, chamfer mill, engraver, thread mills (P0.5–P2) and drills. A cutter&apos;s assembly is its half-section profile from the tip, split into cutting zone, shank and holder.</p>
