  <section id="collision" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Continuous collision checking</h2>
    <p>A controller moves its joints linearly between two points, and a clash can happen between them. <code>checkMotion</code> checks the whole move: probes (the holder and shank, each non-cutting profile segment enclosed by a capsule) against obstacles (the table envelope and the part box). The lower bound is a Lipschitz argument: a signed distance changes by at most the displacement of the moving solid, so the move is bisected until every interval is proven clear by <code>margin</code>, an overlap is sampled, or the depth (12) runs out.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Verdict</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>clear</td><td>on every interval the lower bound of the clearance is at least margin</td></tr>
        <tr><td>collision</td><td>a sampled probe point lies inside an obstacle; at says where along the move (0 to 1)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>unresolved</td><td>an interval could not be proven clear within the depth, and no overlap was found</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCode language="json" title="flat-6 in a linear scene over tableBox(20, 15, −10, 200, 100, 20) — real output">
{"// rapid along X at nose z 60\n{\u0022verdict\u0022:\u0022clear\u0022,\u0022clearance\u0022:26.875,\u0022evaluations\u0022:2}\n// plunge from nose z 60 to nose z 0\n{\u0022verdict\u0022:\u0022collision\u0022,\u0022clearance\u0022:-10,\u0022at\u0022:1,\u0022evaluations\u0022:2}\n// standing poses\nz 60 {\u0022verdict\u0022:\u0022clear\u0022,\u0022clearance\u0022:46.875,\u0022evaluations\u0022:2}\nz 20 {\u0022verdict\u0022:\u0022clear\u0022,\u0022clearance\u0022:6.875,\u0022evaluations\u0022:2}\nz 10 {\u0022verdict\u0022:\u0022unresolved\u0022,\u0022clearance\u0022:-3.125,\u0022evaluations\u0022:4097}\nz  5 {\u0022verdict\u0022:\u0022collision\u0022,\u0022clearance\u0022:-5,\u0022at\u0022:0,\u0022evaluations\u0022:1}"}
    </DocCode>
    <p>The rapid reports 26.875 rather than the standing 46.875 because a moving check certifies a lower bound: it subtracts the displacement over each interval it did not subdivide further. The nose-z-10 pose shows why <code>unresolved</code> exists: the certified lower bound is negative (the envelope might overlap) yet no sampled point is inside, so after 4 097 evaluations the checker reports what it can prove instead of guessing. Envelopes are conservative: a capsule encloses its holder segment, so an overlap can be reported where the real tool would only come close.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="troubleshooting" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>job status failed: Unsupported job schema version</td><td>job built for a different kernel schema</td><td>rebuild the job with the current schemaVersion</td></tr>
        <tr><td>remaining-volume bracket wide</td><td>voxel too coarse for the features</td><td>use a smaller voxel; the slack is about one voxel along each wall</td></tr>
        <tr><td>collision on a rapid</td><td>holder or shank envelope reaches the table or part</td><td>raise the clearance plane or shorten the stickout</td></tr>
        <tr><td>unresolved on a long rotary move</td><td>the Lipschitz bound is weak far from the rotation centre</td><td>split the move, or accept the margin the checker could prove</td></tr>
        <tr><td>operation planned with an unexpected cutter</td><td>cutter suitability for the material and rigidity</td><td>pass a tool library with the cutter you want</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

